From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 16:38:42 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 132/148] fix(ci): name a go-install build's source from its
 module's .Origin.Hash (gcp-fooz4)

A `go install pkg@REF` build is module mode and records no
vcs.revision, and the fallback has no release tag. After be578c74 the
fallback therefore always failed, after its full build. That broke
branch and SHA dispatch for pack-release-compatibility, which never
reads the source ref, as well as for the inference gates.

The build info does record the module version REF resolved to: a
release version, or a pseudo-version for a branch or SHA. The module
proxy records that exact version's commit as .Origin.Hash, which is how
upstream's #476 resolved it. The fallback now looks that up with
`go mod download -json` and exports the full SHA. If the lookup comes up
empty, the install still fails loud and exports nothing. Checked against
proxy.golang.org: v1.5.0 gives f66474617d2b..., and the pseudo-version
for main's 164a7b32acd4 gives the full 164a7b32acd4118c....

The go stub answers `mod download -json` with an Origin only for the
exact version the fake build recorded. Tests cover a hash present
(exports it) and a hash absent (fails, naming the version). The doc and
the inference workflows' gascity_ref descriptions now say a branch or
SHA is built and named this way, rather than failing.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
